cowtree
=======

::

     _________
    < cowtree >
     ---------
           \  (__)
            \ (oo)
       /-------\/
      / |     ||
     *  ||----||
        ~~    ~~

Give each agent a Git worktree so its edits do not collide with yours.
Ordinary worktrees write another copy of the tracked files, even when the
agent changes only one.

Cowtree shares unchanged file storage through filesystem copy-on-write.
Each worktree has its own files and Git index. Writing in one allocates
private storage for the changed data and leaves the others untouched.

Start
-----

Git and a filesystem with native cloning. Build the native CLI from this
checkout with Rust 1.85+::

    cargo install --locked --path crates/cli
    cowtree doctor .
    cowtree add -b scratch/idea ../wt/idea
    cowtree list --json
    cowtree remove ../wt/idea

The result is an ordinary Git worktree. Agents can edit, build, commit, and
open a pull request with their existing tools. Nothing to feed.

macOS uses clonefile, Linux uses FICLONE, and Windows uses block cloning on
capable volumes such as ReFS. Source and destination must share a filesystem.
``doctor`` tests actual cloning and write isolation, not just a filesystem name.
Unsupported volumes fail before creating a worktree.

For agents
----------

The installer also provides ``git-cowtree``, so agents can invoke everything
through Git::

    git cowtree add --committed -b agent/fix ../wt/fix HEAD
    git cowtree list --json
    git cowtree remove ../wt/fix

Change the agent's worktree command template to ``git cowtree``. Its other
Git commands stay the same. ``--committed`` starts from the selected commit
while preserving uncommitted edits in the source checkout.

See `agent setup <docs/agents.rst>`_ for a repo-local ``git wt`` alias,
an instruction block for agents, and the differences from ``git worktree``.

Measured space
--------------

Linux v6.12, 86,680 tracked files, four extra worktrees on APFS::

    State                  Git GiB    cowtree GiB    Reduction
    Pristine                 6.464          0.163       97.47%
    After 1% atomic saves     6.466          0.238       96.32%
    After two churn rounds   6.473          0.223       96.55%

These are additional-worktree allocations, including filesystem metadata and
Git indexes. This is one paired trial at revision ``f97d7cc``. It establishes
space savings for that workload. Including the shared source, the after-edit
saving was 74.71%. Full inventories matched and the source remained unchanged.

`Figure, recorded evidence, and method <docs/space-efficiency.rst>`_.
`Benchmark methods <benchmarks/README.rst>`_ distinguish historical physical
allocation evidence from the native creation benchmark.

Quick API
---------

Standalone commands clone tracked files, not ignored build caches::

    cowtree add [-b NEW | --branch EXISTING | --detach]
                [--committed] [--lock] [--reason TEXT] [--json] PATH [REF]
    cowtree list [--json] [SOURCE]
    cowtree remove [--force] [--json] PATH
    cowtree doctor [--json] [DIRECTORY]
    cowtree --version [--json]

``add`` defaults to detached HEAD and requires a clean source checkout.
``--committed`` materializes a pinned ref without changing the caller's edits.
``--branch`` attaches an unused local branch. ``-b`` creates one. Removal keeps
the branch. Add ``--help`` for options or ``--`` before option-like paths.

Uninitialized submodules remain empty by default. Initialized children require
``--submodules materialize-pinned`` to create independent child repositories at
their pinned commits. ``--submodules reject`` retains blanket refusal.
See `standalone submodules <docs/standalone-submodules.rst>`_ for local-object
requirements, Git configuration, and cleanup semantics.

Rust operations live in ``libcowtree``. For example:

.. code-block:: rust

    use cowtree::{AddRequest, Branch, add_worktree};

    let mut request = AddRequest::new("../wt/idea".into());
    request.branch = Branch::New("scratch/idea".into());
    let tree = add_worktree(&request)?;

Failures return typed errors. CLI ``--json`` failures go to stderr. Exit status:
0 success, 1 operation failure, 2 invalid syntax. See the
`standalone contract <docs/standalone.rst>`_ for ownership and cleanup limits.

The same Rust CLI also manages build caches, checkpoints, checked publication,
and recovery::

    cowtree workspace --root /absolute/store init \
        --source /absolute/project --derived target
    cowtree workspace --root /absolute/store fork /absolute/worktrees/task

``cowtree-workspace`` exposes these workflows as Rust methods. Its sessions call
``cowtree-metadata`` directly. A native supervisor keeps validation bounded when
the coordinating process exits. Git remains a subprocess dependency.

Start with the `Cargo quickstart <docs/cargo-workspaces.rst#first-workspace>`_
or the `managed API <docs/managed-workspaces.rst>`_. Managed Windows support is
not implemented. Qualification remains for opt-in developer use.

Where to look
-------------

`docs/install.rst <docs/install.rst>`_
    Build and install the native executable.
`docs/performance.rst <docs/performance.rst>`_
    Import, fresh forks, prepared paths, and compiler reuse measured separately.
`docs/filesystems.rst <docs/filesystems.rst>`_
    Native filesystem tests and the support matrix.
`docs/production-readiness.rst <docs/production-readiness.rst>`_
    Deployment gates and what the current evidence does not establish.
`crates/workspace/ <crates/workspace/README.md>`_
    Managed filesystem coordination and recovery.
`crates/libcowtree/ <crates/libcowtree/README.md>`_
    Required Rust engine for standalone operations and snapshot trees.
`crates/git/ <crates/git/README.md>`_
    Shared synchronous Git execution and explicit lock inheritance.
`crates/cli/ <crates/cli/>`_
    Native cowtree and git-cowtree executables. No Python runtime.
`crates/metadata/ <crates/metadata/README.md>`_
    Rust authority linked into managed operations, with a diagnostic JSON-line CLI.
Tests live beside their implementation or under each crate's ``tests/`` directory.
    Command contracts, authority integration, and native filesystem invariants.
`specs/ <specs/>`_
    Executable protocol models. See `verification <docs/verification.rst>`_ for limits.
`benchmarks/ <benchmarks/README.rst>`_
    Native creation benchmark and historical measurement procedures.
`CONTRIBUTING.rst <CONTRIBUTING.rst>`_
    Setup, checks, and contributor access. MIT license. See `LICENSE <LICENSE>`_.
